  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>The Vallon Sourn ("dark" in Provençal) is a 3 km gorge carved by the Argens river through the limestone plateau, between the villages of Châteauvert and Correns, right in the heart of the Provence Verte. The cliffs framing it, up to 40 m high, make it a genuine haven of coolness in summer, with natural shade that's rare in this otherwise very sun-exposed region. The site is confirmed by the Provence Verte & Verdon tourist office, Var Tourisme, the PACA regional tourism board and numerous Tripadvisor reviews.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>Two "wild beaches" let you swim in the Argens, one on the Correns side and one on the Châteauvert side, both unsupervised — swimming is at users' own risk, with parental supervision required for minors. Dogs are allowed and must be kept on a leash; no source mentions a ban on dog swimming here (unlike nearby Lac de Saint-Cassien or Lac de Carcès, which are drinking-water reserves where all swimming is banned). The ground is made up of limestone slabs and rocky patches, worth watching if your dog has sensitive paws. Free parking is at Mas de Bagarèdes on the D45, 1.5 km from Châteauvert; then allow about 800 m on foot to reach the main beach.</p>
      <p>Two real points of caution to know about: the flow of the Argens is very seasonal (average minimum flow around 6 m³/s in August, which can drop to 2.4 m³/s during droughts), so the water level can be noticeably lower in high summer without necessarily being dry. Also, swimming has already been temporarily banned by municipal order in Correns (August 2024) following an unfavourable water test, before reopening after a new test — a good habit is to check the swimming status with the Correns or Châteauvert town hall, or the tourist office, before going in high season.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: the gorge's cliffs make it one of the rare waterways in the Var where your dog can enjoy real continuous shade even at the height of summer — a real plus compared to the more exposed beaches or lakes in the area.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>The villages of Correns (France's first organic village) and Châteauvert, as well as the gorges themselves, are also popular for climbing (250 equipped routes) and canoeing upstream.</p>
